Define φ(r) = A(r)/B(r) = F(r). Alternatively, use the standard trick: A(r) = ∫_0^r f = ∫_0^r (f/g) g. So F(r) = ∫_0^r w g / ∫_0^r g where w = f/g is non-increasing. The classical Chebyshev/mean value lemma: the weighted average of a non-increasing function w.r.t. a positive measure is non-increasing as the measure grows. Proof: F(r) = ∫ w dμ_r where dμ_r = g ds/∫_0^r g ds is a probability measure. For r₂ > r₁: μ_{r₂} = (B₁/B₂) μ_{r₁} + ((B₂-B₁)/B₂) ν where ν = (g ds restricted to [r₁,r₂])/∫_{r₁}^{r₂} g. Then F(r₂) = (B₁/B₂)F(r₁) + ((B₂-B₁)/B₂)∫ w dν ≤ (B₁/B₂)F(r₁) + ((B₂-B₁)/B₂) w(r₁) ≤ F(r₁) since w(r₁) ≤ F(r₁)?? Hmm: F(r₁) = ∫ w dμ_{r₁} ≥ w(r₁) since w non-increasing and μ_{r₁} supported on [0,r₁]: indeed w(s) ≥ w(r₁) for s ≤ r₁. So F(r₂) ≤ (B₁/B₂)F(r₁) + ((B₂-B₁)/B₂)F(r₁) = F(r₁). 

This proof uses measure theory (interval integral as measure) — could be done via integral additivity and monotonicity. In Lean this is doable: F(r₂) = A₂/B₂ = (A₁ + ∫_{r₁}^{r₂} f)/(B₁ + ∫_{r₁}^{r₂} g). And ∫_{r₁}^{r₂} f ≤ ∫_{r₁}^{r₂} (f(r₁)/g(r₁)) g = (f(r₁)/g(r₁)) ∫_{r₁}^{r₂} g using f(s)/g(s) ≤ f(r₁)/g(r₁) for s ∈ [r₁, r₂]. Then algebra: A₂/B₂ ≤ A₁/B₁ ⟺ (A₁ + ΔA)/(B₁+ΔB) ≤ A₁/B₁ ⟺ B₁ ΔA ≤ A₁ ΔB. And ΔA ≤ (f(r₁)/g(r₁)) ΔB = w(r₁) ΔB, and w(r₁) B₁ ≤ A₁ (since A₁ = ∫_0^{r₁} f ≥ ∫_0^{r₁} (f(r₁)/g(r₁)) g = w(r₁) B₁). So B₁ ΔA ≤ B₁ w(r₁) ΔB ≤ A₁ ΔB. 

